# Seeds for failure cases proptest has generated in the past. It is
# automatically read and these particular cases re-run before any
# novel cases are generated.
#
# It is recommended to check this file in to source control so that
# everyone who runs the test benefits from these saved cases.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A `cc` line is a seed for the generator, not a recording of a source string.
# It reproduces the ORIGINAL failing case; the comment records what that case
# SHRANK to. And it decodes through the generators AS THEY ARE NOW, so changing
# `arb_soup`, `arb_task_tree`, `arb_mutation` or `arb_eol` re-maps the random
# stream and every line here stops reaching the input it names.
#
# All three below are good: e6fb855 only ADDED `arb_archive_model` and the
# archive properties, leaving the existing generators untouched. Its seeds are
# replayed against every property in this file, which is proptest's per-file
# behaviour and is harmless.
#
# BEFORE changing an existing generator, convert anything load-bearing here into
# a fixed-case test.
#
# One more thing a seed used to not survive: the machine. The mutation properties
# pick a fixture with `which % corpus.len()`, and `fixture_sources` read the
# directory in `read_dir` order — unspecified, and different on CI than locally.
# A seed from a CI failure therefore decoded to a different fixture here. The
# corpus is sorted by file name as of this commit, so seeds are portable from now
# on; any recorded before it is not.
#
# `arb_mutation` HAS now been changed: its `prop_oneof!` arms are weighted by
# cardinality, so that all 42 mutations are equally likely rather than each
# `Replace(n)` drawing 5x less often than `Delete`. That took the mutation
# properties from 38% to 91% per run at no runtime cost — see `MUTATION_CASES`
# in parse_properties.rs.
#
# It was done at the one moment it was free: no mutation seed was live. The CI
# seed below was already `found:`, and its case is pinned as a fixed test. So the
# usual cost of touching a generator — orphaning seeds that silently keep passing
# as something else — was not being paid by anything.
#
# The three seeds below are NOT orphaned by it. Each names a case from `arb_soup`
# or `arb_archive_model`, and the tests those seeds are for never draw from
# `arb_mutation`, so their streams are untouched. (They are also replayed against
# the mutation properties, per proptest's per-file behaviour; those replays do
# re-map, and never named anything.) The second seed's case has a fixed pin
# either way, in `a_stranded_run_settles_in_one_write`.
#
# found: cc 3865415bc19bec00e1387474e51e8d0395c0a8ade4ef867791be5af2baac3c52
#   P2 in CI, on the unsorted corpus: which = 32, idx = 276, mutation =
#   Replace(15) — `code_in_notes.md` with the first block's closing fence
#   replaced by an `added:` line. Not re-listed as a live seed, because it
#   predates the sort and no longer decodes to that case. Pinned instead by
#   `an_unclosed_fence_cut_short_by_metadata_settles_without_loss`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
